  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "0.1.1: database names are matched in any case, as SQL Server matches them. In 0.1.0 a policy, or a flush, had to spell the database exactly as the session did: a session spelling it Sales was not cached under a policy for SALES, and EXEC daffyzoom.flush 'SALES' did not end its answers. Now policies, flushes and writes seen all match in any case; cached answers stay apart by spelling, for case-sensitive servers. db set says when no login's target is on the server it names (SERVER is the SQL Server, not a DaffyZoom login), as do the startup banner and daffyzoom.databases; the banner says when no database is cached yet, with a db set line made from a login's target. The watch list gives the db set line for a query whose database has no policy; daffyzoom.flush says when no policy caches the database; daffyzoom.status and :sessions show the database a session is in now, not the one it logged in to. A db set that fails part-way no longer changes the policy a running DaffyZoom serves by. Whitespace that cannot change an answer is no longer part of a batch's key: spaces, tabs and line breaks at its end (SSMS's line before a GO, a blank line at the end of the window), and spaces or tabs before its first line; line breaks before it still count, since they move the line numbers in messages. New --log-retention, on by default at 24h: log files (--log-file) and transcripts (--transcript) are deleted once nothing has been written to them for that long, and a log file without {date} is moved to NAME.old and started afresh; off keeps them. More db set examples on this page.\n\n0.1.0: first release. A result cache in front of SQL Server and Azure SQL, as a gateway: slow queries that repeat exactly are answered from an encrypted local copy, everything else is relayed byte for byte, and whenever DaffyZoom is unsure the query goes to SQL Server. Nothing is cached until a database has a policy: ttl mode (max age, a flush from the ETL job, a watermark poll, scheduled flushes, blackout windows, writes seen through DaffyZoom) or tracked mode (Change Tracking checked on every hit, as the client's own login). Prepared statements (sp_prepexec) are served with a handle SQL Server issued for the client's own session. Administration in T-SQL (EXEC daffyzoom.help), including daffyzoom.tracking, which shows Change Tracking as the caller's login sees it and the GRANT that fixes what is missing. The cache keeps within --cache-max-gb and what its disk can spare. Checked end to end against SQL Server 2022 with twelve drivers.",
